      <div class="faq-container">

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              Why do cats purr?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>Cats purr for a variety of reasons beyond simple contentment. They purr when happy, but also when stressed, injured, or even giving birth. The vibration frequency (25–150 Hz) has been linked to tissue healing and pain relief, suggesting purring may serve a self-soothing and therapeutic purpose as well as a social one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              Why do cats knock things off surfaces?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>Cats are natural hunters with a strong instinct to bat, paw, and investigate objects. Pushing things off ledges lets them practice hunting motions, test object permanence, and — let's be honest — get your attention. Cats quickly learn that knocking over your coffee mug is a very effective way to summon you.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              How many hours a day do cats sleep?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>On average, cats sleep between 12 and 16 hours a day, though some can clock up to 20 hours. As crepuscular hunters (most active at dawn and dusk), cats conserve energy during the day and night with frequent naps. Kittens and senior cats sleep even more than adults.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              Why do cats bring their owners dead animals?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>This behaviour comes from a cat's maternal and social instincts. Mother cats teach kittens to hunt by bringing them prey. When your cat brings you a "gift", they're treating you as a member of their family and sharing their bounty. It's a sign of trust and affection — even if you'd rather they didn't.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              Do cats actually have nine lives?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>No — cats have one life, just like other mammals. The myth likely arose from their remarkable survival abilities: a flexible skeleton, a righting reflex that helps them land on their feet, and the ability to survive falls that would injure larger animals. The number nine has long held symbolic significance in many cultures, adding to the legend.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              Why do cats rub against people and objects?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>This behaviour is called "bunting" or scent marking. Cats have scent glands on their cheeks, forehead, chin, and paws. When they rub against you or the furniture, they're depositing their scent and claiming the item or person as part of their territory. It's also a social bonding gesture — a cat rubbing against you is saying "you're mine, and I'm yours."</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              Can cats see in the dark?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>Cats can't see in complete darkness, but they see far better in low light than humans can. Their eyes have a high rod-to-cone ratio and a reflective layer called the <em>tapetum lucidum</em> that amplifies available light. This is also why cats' eyes glow in photos or when caught in a torch beam at night.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              Why do cats knead with their paws?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>Kneading — the rhythmic push-pull motion cats make with their front paws — starts in kittenhood when kittens knead their mother's belly to stimulate milk flow. Adult cats retain the behaviour as a comfort mechanism. A cat kneading on you is a sign of contentment and security; consider it a high compliment.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
