          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              How many hours a day do cats sleep?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>On average, cats sleep between 12 and 16 hours a day, though some can clock up to 20 hours. As crepuscular hunters (most active at dawn and dusk), cats conserve energy during the day and night with frequent naps. Kittens and senior cats sleep even more than adults.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              Why do cats bring their owners dead animals?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>This behaviour comes from a cat's maternal and social instincts. Mother cats teach kittens to hunt by bringing them prey. When your cat brings you a "gift", they're treating you as a member of their family and sharing their bounty. It's a sign of trust and affection — even if you'd rather they didn't.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="example">
          <h2>
            <button type="button" aria-expanded="true" class="faq-trigger">
              Do cats actually have nine lives?
            </button>
          </h2>
          <div class="details">
            <p>No — cats have one life, just like other mammals. The myth likely arose from their remarkable survival abilities: a flexible skeleton, a righting reflex that helps them land on their feet, and the ability to survive falls that would injure larger animals. The number nine has long held symbolic significance in many cultures, adding to the legend.</p>
