            <p>This behaviour is called "bunting" or scent marking. Cats have scent glands on their cheeks, forehead, chin, and paws. When they rub against you or the furniture, they're depositing their scent and claiming the item or person as part of their territory. It's also a social bonding gesture — a cat rubbing against you is saying "you're mine, and I'm yours."</p>
